#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c023b634-e106-5fbc-a8ff-197f3aa4869d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Geometric Sequence Converges Given the General Term"}] :question/id #uuid "f6c910a1-cd2c-5d9a-ad67-974aaba4a5b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-647" :question/problem "Does the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac {2 \\cdot (-3) ^ n}{5 ^ {n + 1}}$ for $n\\geq 1$ converge or diverge?\nIf it converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form\n\n$$\na_n = a \\cdot r^{n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= (2 \\cdot (-3)^{n})/(5^{n + 1}) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (-3)^{n})/(5 \\cdot 5^{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot ((-3)^{n})/(5^{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(-\\frac{3}{5})^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now see that this is the geometric sequence with\n\n$$\nr=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to $0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "126485fb-1b7f-5602-bc8d-05908ac6f37c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges"}] :question/id #uuid "77fc1164-55b1-5f37-a5af-02098da2a2ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-649" :question/problem "Consider the geometric sequence\n$a_n =\\dfrac{x^2}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\right)^{n}, \\qquad n\\geq 1$\n\nwhere $x$ is a positive real number. For which values of $x$ is the sequence convergent?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{16}{x^2}\n$$\n\nSince $x^2$ is positive, $r$ is also positive, So, the convergence condition is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt r\\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0 \\lt\\dfrac{16}{x^2}\\leq 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x^2 \\geq 16\n$$\n\nTaking into account the fact that $x$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\nx \\geq 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abad9fa9-b426-50aa-aaa7-d472ea974f98" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Values for Which a Geometric Sequence Converges: Rearrangement Required"}] :question/id #uuid "ff1b9ef5-e99a-5252-b6d2-8981f2f9938f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9990" :question/problem "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}}$, where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1$. What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form\n\n$$\na_n = a \\cdot r^{n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= \\frac{x^{2n + 1}}{x^{n - 2}} \\\\\n&= x^{2n + 1 - n + 2} \\\\\n&= x^{n + 3} \\\\\n&= x^{3} \\cdot x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x$. Since $x$ is positive, $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt r \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x \\leq 1\n$$"}]}
